;; How the pin set of ADR-0044 reaches a device, and how it changes.
;;
;; An anchor set is a signed, sequenced document that changes what the machine
;; will trust, so it is admitted by aiueos.publisher like a release -- same four
;; attacks, same answers, no second weaker copy. What is added here is what is
;; true of anchor sets and of nothing else: an empty set is a brick, replacing
;; every anchor at once is a one-way door, and a device with no anchors cannot
;; be handed one over a connection it has no way to judge.
[{:db/id -1
  :aiueos.anchors/id :aiueos/default-anchors
  :aiueos.anchors/source-files ["src/grant/anchors.cljk"]
  :aiueos.anchors/composes ["src/grant/publisher.cljk"]
  :aiueos.anchors/consumed-by ["src/grant/cloud.cljk" "src/aiueos/provider/cloud.cljk"]
  :aiueos.anchors/set-fields [:set-id :sequence :anchors :signatures :timestamp-ms
                              :digest-matches? :break-glass? :bootstrap?]
  :aiueos.anchors/decision-shapes #{:grant :deny}
  :aiueos.anchors/deny-reasons #{:set-id-missing :anchor-set-empty :no-current-set
                                 :disjoint-without-break-glass
                                 :break-glass-below-threshold
                                 :no-anchors-artifact :anchors-for-another-release
                                 :anchors-digest-mismatch
                                 :document-malformed :document-version-unknown
                                 :document-pin-malformed}
  :aiueos.anchors/reasons-pass-through
  "aiueos.publisher reasons are returned unchanged; a caller reading a verdict
   should not have to guess which layer refused"
  :aiueos.anchors/empty-set :denied
  :aiueos.anchors/disjoint-set :break-glass-only
  :aiueos.anchors/break-glass {:threshold :every-root-key
                               :drops-previous-immediately? true
                               :verdict-flag :aiueos.anchors/one-way?}
  :aiueos.anchors/first-set :in-the-image
  :aiueos.anchors/release-borne
  {:artifact-kind :anchors
   :set-id :the-artifact-digest-the-manifest-binds
   :bound-to-release-by :release-id
   :bootstrap-decided-by :device-state-not-the-document
   :bypasses-anchor-rules? false
   :note "from-release builds a proposal; admit-set judges it. A release that
          replaces every anchor is refused exactly as a standalone set is --
          a perfectly signed release that strands the fleet is still a
          stranded fleet."}
  :aiueos.anchors/document
  {:version 1
   :fields [:anchors/version :anchors/release-id :anchors/sequence
            :anchors/anchors :anchors/break-glass?]
   :signature-key :anchors/signature
   :canonicaliser "aiueos.key-lifecycle/document-bytes"
   :canonicaliser-note "the one this repository already has; a second would be
                        a signature-confusion hazard"
   :pins :sorted
   :pin-form "64 lowercase hex characters, refused otherwise -- a malformed pin
              can never match a measured key, so it would arrive looking
              entirely valid and partly not work"
   :guest-path "/etc/aiueos/anchors.edn"
   :named-in-boot-edn :aiueos/anchors
   :staged-bytes :the-exact-bytes-that-were-digested
   :read-at-boot-by "src/aiueos/pid1.cljk"
   :boot-outcomes {:absent "boots with no pins; :aiueos.anchors/present? false"
                   :named-and-unreadable "refuses to boot"
                   :named-and-valid ":aiueos.cloud/trust-anchors is set"}
   :boot-provenance :image
   :boot-provenance-note
   "PID 1 loads and validates; it does not re-admit. The document's integrity
    is the image's integrity -- re-checking a publisher signature at PID-1 time
    adds nothing when the image was verified and cannot repair anything when it
    was not, because PID 1 is already running that image's code."}
  :aiueos.anchors/sequence-key :installed-anchor-sequence
  :aiueos.anchors/sequence-key-note
  "Anchor sets and releases are both sequenced and both checked by
   aiueos.publisher, which reads :installed-sequence. One shared key means the
   higher number silently blocks the other stream and a rollback arrives
   looking like an upgrade, so anchor state carries its own."
  :aiueos.anchors/trust-on-first-use :refused
  :aiueos.anchors/overlap-window-ms 604800000
  :aiueos.anchors/freshness-gates :admission-only
  :aiueos.anchors/assurance-gaps
  ["Identity is supplied, not computed. :set-id is a digest the caller made and
    :digest-matches? is the caller's claim about it; this namespace guarantees
    the same id flows through the decision, not that the id is right. Same
    boundary as aiueos.ota's :manifest-id."
   "Nothing here fetches or verifies a signature. :signatures carries the
    provider's per-signature :verified? answer, as everywhere else in this
    repository."
   "The HOSTED image carries the set: aiueos.image stages it and names its
    digest. The BARE-METAL release image does not -- os/aiueos's
    build-release-image.sh emits no :anchors artifact -- so a bare-metal device
    still has no anchors."
   "The hosted boot path inherits whatever verified the image, which today is
    nothing: the bare-metal profile has measured Secure Boot, the hosted one
    does not. PID 1 reading the file correctly cannot substitute for that."
   "The document is not itself signed. A release-borne set does not need to be
    -- the manifest binds its digest and the manifest is signed -- but a set
    delivered any other way currently has no integrity of its own."
   "No pin for kotobase.net or api.murakumo.cloud is recorded anywhere. This
    decides how a set changes, not what the first one contains."
   "Rotation is modelled, not exercised: no fleet has run add -> switch ->
    retire against a real endpoint, and the overlap window has never been
    measured against how long convergence actually takes."]}]
